WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:08.601
DG32-2DOM is the attention variant of Deepgrid Semi's DG32-LITE motor-control chip.

2
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:18.808
It keeps the dual-core lockstep controller and adds an INT8 attention engine on its own clock, so a drive can watch its own motor for faults.

3
00:00:19.358 --> 00:00:22.842
The design is complete and in physical trials.

4
00:00:23.392 --> 00:00:29.620
Every figure here is a post-route, simulated or analytic value, and each one is labelled.

5
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.115
Here is the answer on one slide.

6
00:00:33.665 --> 00:00:39.862
DG32-2DOM is DG32-LITE plus an attention engine, not a new core.

7
00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:46.160
The lockstep CPU pair, boot path, peripherals and sixty-four-pin pinout are identical.

8
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:57.819
The engine runs at one hundred fourteen megahertz, its output is bit-exact to the software model, and one query row costs about thirty-two hundred cycles, by analysis.

9
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.529
Three findings forced the design.

10
00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:09.766
The lockstep core tops out around fifty-five to sixty-two megahertz, so the control domain has to stay at fifty.

11
00:01:10.316 --> 00:01:15.163
The engine is the throughput consumer, so it gets its own faster clock.

12
00:01:15.713 --> 00:01:24.419
And adding its memory to the DG32-LITE floorplan made routing time out, so the variant gets a larger die of its own.

13
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.344
This is the architecture.

14
00:01:27.894 --> 00:01:33.354
On the left, the fifty megahertz control domain: everything in DG32-LITE.

15
00:01:33.904 --> 00:01:40.963
On the right, the one hundred fourteen megahertz compute domain: the attention engine and its key and value buffers.

16
00:01:41.513 --> 00:01:44.384
Between them sit three clock-domain bridges.

17
00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:50.637
The engine reaches memory only through those bridges, so it can never stall the control core.

18
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.356
Everything proven on DG32-LITE carries over unchanged.

19
00:01:56.906 --> 00:02:06.022
The lockstep pair, fault latch, boot ROM, SRAM, motor drive, sensing and connectivity are the same blocks from the same design source.

20
00:02:06.572 --> 00:02:17.109
The control loop still costs about three hundred hardware cycles, and the package is the same nine-by-nine millimetre QFN, because the engine adds no pads.

21
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:23.078
One kick computes a band of query rows of one attention head, end to end.

22
00:02:23.628 --> 00:02:25.375
Firmware programs the shapes.

23
00:02:25.925 --> 00:02:30.071
A shared multiply array computes Q times K transpose.

24
00:02:30.621 --> 00:02:34.263
A row maximum indexes a table of softmax weights.

25
00:02:34.813 --> 00:02:42.878
The engine forms the weighted sum, divides once per row, saturates the result to INT8, and writes the output back to memory.

26
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.062
Why INT8, and not INT4?

27
00:02:46.612 --> 00:02:52.149
With about four hundred similar keys, each softmax weight is close to one four-hundredth.

28
00:02:52.699 --> 00:02:57.332
INT4 rounds that to zero, and the output came out identically zero.

29
00:02:57.882 --> 00:03:08.930
The engine keeps fifteen-bit weights all the way into a forty-bit numerator, which stays exact to more than one hundred thousand keys, and it saturates only the final output.

30
00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:14.385
Memory traffic decides whether the engine fights the rest of the chip.

31
00:03:14.935 --> 00:03:23.135
Keys and values load once per kick and are re-read for every query row, which keeps one head's bus traffic around one percent.

32
00:03:23.685 --> 00:03:28.499
Streaming the values row by row would cost about four hundred times that.

33
00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:36.240
A DMA and the SRAM's idle read port can feed the engine without contending with the CPU.

34
00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.668
Crossings between the two clocks are rare: keys and values load once, and outputs write back once.

35
00:03:44.218 --> 00:03:50.763
So the bridges use a small four-phase handshake through two-flop synchronisers instead of a FIFO.

36
00:03:51.313 --> 00:04:00.467
One bridge carries programming, one transaction at a time, and two burst bridges batch a whole read or a whole write into a single crossing.

37
00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:10.749
Here is what one query row costs, by analysis, at sixteen lanes, four hundred keys, thirty-two-byte keys and sixty-four-byte values.

38
00:04:11.299 --> 00:04:15.514
The value pass is the largest share, at sixteen hundred cycles.

39
00:04:16.064 --> 00:04:22.025
The key pass takes eight hundred, requantisation about seven hundred, and the divide forty-eight.

40
00:04:22.575 --> 00:04:26.330
That totals about thirty-two hundred and forty cycles.

41
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.142
The measured figure comes at bring-up.

42
00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.512
Both domains close timing on the larger die.

43
00:04:34.062 --> 00:04:44.587
The fifty megahertz control domain closes with zero point three nanoseconds of slack, and the one hundred fourteen megahertz compute domain with zero point one nine.

44
00:04:45.137 --> 00:04:48.876
The die is three point four by four point five millimetres.

45
00:04:49.426 --> 00:04:53.432
These are post-route results; silicon measurements follow bring-up.

46
00:04:54.630 --> 00:05:03.742
The engine is designed for condition monitoring inside the drive: bearing-fault and anomaly detection, on the chip that already turns the motor.

47
00:05:04.292 --> 00:05:11.145
Against the STM32G0, which runs AI in software, that is a hardware differentiator.

48
00:05:11.695 --> 00:05:19.582
It sits on the roadmap alongside DG32-LITE first silicon, with its own design complete and in physical trials.

49
00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.632
Three things to take away.

50
00:05:23.182 --> 00:05:28.066
The engine is an addition, not a redesign: the safety core is untouched.

51
00:05:28.616 --> 00:05:35.142
Its arithmetic is exact: INT8 output, bit-exact to the model, with no weight rounded to zero.

52
00:05:35.692 --> 00:05:41.326
And its cost is known before silicon: about thirty-two hundred cycles per query row.

53
00:05:41.876 --> 00:05:45.707
First silicon turns these design values into measurements.

